-- title:
--   Theorem 16, p. 18 — repeated double auctions: bounded, CS(x^t) ↓ 0 with CS(x^t) ≤ (1/t) Σ_i (u_i(x^t_i) − u_i(x^0_i))/δ_i; cluster points individually rational equilibria (Pareto under Assumption 4)
-- statement:
--   Let $I$ be a finite nonempty set of agents and $J$ a finite set of assets, with utilities $u_i:\mathbb R^J\to[-\infty,+\infty]$, numeraire $g$ and initial allocation $x^0$. Assume Assumptions 1, 3 and 5. Let $(x^t)_{t\ge0}$ be generated by repeated double auctions: for each $t\ge1$, the purchases $\bar x^t$ and the market clearing price $p^t$ satisfy $\sum_i\bar x^t_i=0$ and $p^t\in\partial D_i^{t}(\bar x^t_i)$ for all $i$, where $D^t_i$ is agent $i$'s indifference price at $x^{t-1}_i$, and
--   $$x^t_i=x^{t-1}_i+\bar x^t_i-(p^t\cdot\bar x^t_i)g.$$
--   Then:
--
--   1. the sequence $(x^t)$ is bounded;
--   2. $CS(x^t)$ is nonincreasing in $t$ and converges to $0$;
--   3. there is $R>0$ such that, whenever $\delta_i>0$ satisfy the inequality of Assumption 5 at radius $R$ ($u_i(z+Rg)\ge u_i(z)+\delta_iR$ for $z\in\mathbb B(R)$), for all $t\ge1$
--   $$CS(x^t)\le\frac1t\sum_{i\in I}\frac{u_i(x^t_i)-u_i(x^0_i)}{\delta_i};$$
--   4. every cluster point $\bar x$ of $(x^t)$ is a double auction equilibrium ($CS(\bar x)=0$) and individually rational ($u_i(\bar x_i)\ge u_i(x^0_i)$ for all $i$);
--   5. if moreover every $u_i$ satisfies Assumption 4, every cluster point $\bar x$ is Pareto efficient (among allocations with $\sum_ix_i=\sum_ix^0_i$).
--
--   This is the main result of the paper: repeating the double auction with price-taking, indifference-pricing agents drives the market to an efficient allocation, with the remaining total surplus decaying like $1/t$.
--
--   **Formalization Note** The sequence is any one the mechanism generates: purchases and prices satisfying (2)–(3) at the current allocation; existence of solutions and prices (Theorems 4 and 10) is not part of the statement, and Assumption 2 is not assumed. The values $u_i(x^t_i)$, $u_i(x^0_i)$ are finite under the hypotheses (a proof obligation, not an assumption); the bound is stated with their real values, so no extended-real junk value enters it. The constants $\delta_i$ are those of Assumption 5 at one radius $R$ chosen large enough for the iterates and the surpluses, as in the paper's proof; the statement holds for every admissible choice of such $\delta_i$. $CS$ takes values in $[-\infty,+\infty]$ and convergence is in that space. $\mathbb B(R)$ is the closed sup-norm ball.
-- source:
--   T. Pennanen, Efficient allocations in double auction markets, arXiv:2001.02071v2, p. 18, Theorem 16 (recursion p. 17, Assumption 5 p. 17, individual rationality p. 18; proof pp. 18–19)

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_DoubleAuctionEff_Repeated_Model

namespace DoubleAuctionEff.Repeated
theorem theorem_16 {I J : Type*} [Fintype I] [DecidableEq I] [Nonempty I] [Fintype J] [DecidableEq J] (u : I → (J → ℝ) → EReal) (g : J → ℝ) (x0 : I → J → ℝ) (h1 : ∀ i, Assumption1 (u i) g) (h3 : Assumption3 u) (h5 : ∀ i, Assumption5 (u i) g) (x y : ℕ → I → J → ℝ) (p : ℕ → J → ℝ) (hseq : IsAuctionSeq u g x0 x y p) : Bornology.IsBounded (Set.range x) ∧ Antitone (fun t => CS u g (x t)) ∧ Filter.Tendsto (fun t => CS u g (x t)) Filter.atTop (nhds 0) ∧ (∃ R : ℝ, 0 < R ∧ ∀ δ : I → ℝ, (∀ i, 0 < δ i ∧ Assumption5At (u i) g R (δ i)) → ∀ t : ℕ, 1 ≤ t → CS u g (x t) ≤ (((1 / (t : ℝ)) * ∑ i, ((u i (x t i)).toReal - (u i (x0 i)).toReal) / δ i : ℝ) : EReal)) ∧ (∀ xbar : I → J → ℝ, MapClusterPt xbar Filter.atTop x → IsDAEquilibrium u g xbar ∧ IsIndivRational u x0 xbar) ∧ ((∀ i, Assumption4 (u i) g) → ∀ xbar : I → J → ℝ, MapClusterPt xbar Filter.atTop x → IsParetoEfficient u x0 xbar) := by sorry
end DoubleAuctionEff.Repeated
